Q: What exactly does the Comma operator &quot;,&quot; do A: <p>The <code>,</code> operator wraps the expression in an <code>object[]</code>, there is no more magic to it than that. The array is then consumed (due to enumeration) when outputted from the script block or function leaving the <code>IEnumerable</code> / <code>IEnumerator</code> <em>unenumerated</em>.</p>
<p>You can clearly see it if you use <a href="https://github.com/SeeminglyScience/ScriptBlockDisassembler" rel="nofollow noreferrer"><code>ScriptBlockDisassembler</code></a>:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>{ , @(0..10) } | Get-ScriptBlockDisassembly -Minimal

// ScriptBlock.EndBlock
try
{
    Fake.Dynamic&lt;Action&lt;CallSite, object[], Pipe, ExecutionContext&gt;&gt;(PSPipeWriterBinder.Get())(
        new object[]
        {
            (object)Fake.Dynamic&lt;Func&lt;CallSite, object, object[]&gt;&gt;(PSToObjectArrayBinder.Get())(
                ParserOps.RangeOperator((object)0, (object)10))
        },
        funcContext._outputPipe,
        context);
}
catch (FlowControlException)
{
    throw;
}
catch (Exception exception)
{
    ExceptionHandlingOps.CheckActionPreference(funcContext, exception);
}
</code></pre>
<p>Compared without it, the wrapping of <code>new object[] { ... }</code> is gone:</p>
<pre class="lang-cs prettyprint-override"><code>{ @(0..10) } | Get-ScriptBlockDisassembly -Minimal

// ScriptBlock.EndBlock
try
{
    Fake.Dynamic&lt;Action&lt;CallSite, object[], Pipe, ExecutionContext&gt;&gt;(PSPipeWriterBinder.Get())(
        Fake.Dynamic&lt;Func&lt;CallSite, object, object[]&gt;&gt;(PSToObjectArrayBinder.Get())(
            ParserOps.RangeOperator(0, 10)),
        funcContext._outputPipe,
        context);
}
catch (FlowControlException)
{
    throw;
}
catch (Exception exception)
{
    ExceptionHandlingOps.CheckActionPreference(funcContext, exception);
}
</code></pre>

Q: Is it possible to turn off all CSS animations in Chrome? A: <p>From what I know Chrome has no such option.<br />
But, I was able to make something similar using the <a href="https://chrome.google.com/webstore/detail/tampermonkey/dhdgffkkebhmkfjojejmpbldmpobfkfo?hl=en" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Tampermonkey extension</a>.</p>
<p>Simply add the following script to the extension:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>// ==UserScript==
// @name           Animation Stopper
// @description    Stop all CSS animations on every website.
// @author         Ron Zano - https://stackoverflow.com/users/7845797
// @match          *://*/*
// @grant          GM_addStyle
// @run-at         document-end
// ==/UserScript==

GM_addStyle(`
  *, *:before, *:after {
    transition-property: none !important;
    transform: none !important;
    animation: none !important;
  }`
);

console.log(&quot;Animation Stopper ran successfully&quot;);
</code></pre>
<p>Make sure it's enabled at the extensions bar<br />
(Note: it won't work on <code>iframe</code> elements.)</p>
<p>Btw, You can disable the window animation in chrome by adding the <code>--wm-window-animations-disabled</code> command-line flag.</p>
